        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
During the relatively short flights of Gemini and Apollo, researchers identified definite alterations in the physiology of astronauts.  In some cases, the changes affected the way Gemini and Apollo crews were able to perform mission tasks.  Prior to Skylab, researchers speculated that humans might not perform tasks adequately in a microgravity environment over long periods of time.  The primary objective of the time and motion study was to determine how well astronauts could perform specified tasks under weightless conditions over the course of long-duration space flights.  In addition to this hypothesis, the experiment was designed to determine if repetition of a specified task would improve performance time, and if task performance time in flight would be restored to preflight levels late in the mission, assuming an increase earlier in the mission.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
During the Skylab 2, 3 and 4 missions, the study observed nine astronaut test subjects in motion and compared their mobility and dexterity during various activities in weightlessness with similar activities conducted by the same astronauts under Earth conditions.  The activities that were monitored were selected according to a restrictive set of criteria.  It was necessary for the activities to be repetitive and relatively standardized.  At the same time, they needed to be relevant to the mission and the responses not contrived.  Consequently, the activities had to be part of the planned schedule, not added to or modified for the experiment.  
<P>
Activities associated with the preparation and execution of approved medical and scientific experiments met all of these requirements.  Accordingly, data was collected during several life sciences experiments, including M092 (Inflight Lower Body Negative Pressure), M171 (Metabolic Activity), and M093 (Vectorcardiography).   Data were also collected while the astronauts put on and took off the space suits required for extravehicular activity (EVA).
<P>
Data were collected both preflight and inflight for each astronaut.  Preflight, the astronauts practiced each task until little or no assistance was required to perform the task.  The last four or five trials were used as the baseline or control data for comparison with inflight performance.  Inflight data was then compared to the preflight data to determine if the first few inflight task performance trials remained at preflight levels, or if there was a noticeable change in the performance level in flight.
<P>
A 16 mm data acquisition camera was used to obtain color photographs of selected tasks on each Skylab mission.  Each performance trial was recorded from beginning to end.  Depending on the proximity to the test subject, either a standard 10 mm lens or a 5 mm lens with wide angle field-of-view was used.  On Skylab 2, all data were photographed at six frames per second.  On the longer Skylab 3 and 4 missions, data were collected at two frames per second for some activities.  A standard voice recorder was used by the test subject to record start and stop times of each task.
<P>
The film was returned to Earth where a three-level analysis procedure was developed to filter the data into classes according to the depth and detail of the analysis required.  Each task was broken down into several subtasks  or elements and the times for completing the different elements of a given task were determined.
A task element was characterized as follows:<BR>
<LI> basic, if it was necessary to complete the task;
<LI> non-basic, if it could be omitted (e.g., stowage of leg bands used to measure leg volume);
<LI> foreign, if it was unrelated to the task (e.g., crewman stopping the task to take a message);
<LI> wait, if it was a break;
<LI> idle, if it was non-productive (e.g., reviewing progress);
<LI> anomaly, if it was due to a task-related error (e.g., incorrect procedure) or hardware difficulties.</LI>
<P>
The sum of the times associated with the basic elements of the task was calculated from film analysis as well as from voice records. It usually included the time occupied by anomalies but not the times for other task element categories -- non-basic, foreign, waits and idles. The element times served as the fundamental data source for performing analyses such as gross motor dexterity, translation with or without carrying a load, and large versus small mass handling under weightless conditions.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Analysis of the data indicated that the first performance of a task in flight took longer to complete than the corresponding last preflight trial.  The increase in time in flight was noticed in 68% of the cases during Skylab 2.  Although the effect was not so pronounced for the remaining two missions, the results were consistent.  When the results of the three missions were combined, it was observed that 61% of the first inflight trials took longer than the corresponding last preflight trials.
<P>
In addition, tasks that required fine motor activity, such as high eye-hand coordination and dexterity, took longer to perform than tasks requiring medium or gross motor activity.  It was shown that in many cases, by the second inflight trial, the crewmembers adjusted rapidly to the weightless environment and became proficient in developing techniques to optimize task performance.  For short missions, the researchers concluded that the early repetition of tasks critical for mission success would seem to be the most effective 
allocation of inflight work activities.   
<P>
One interesting result indicated that the length of time necessary to readapt to preflight levels of performance was between one and two weeks.  There was some evidence to indicate that number of trials was more important than mission days in the evaluation of adaptation to task or work performance.  It was observed that by the second trial, whether performed on the same day or a week later, the time tended to approximate that obtained on the last preflight trial. 
<P>
Finally, there was no significant evidence to indicate deterioration of performance during the longer Skylab missions.  It was shown that performance continued to improve while variability did not increase significantly during the final third of the Skylab mission.
<END>
